{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>For it was uncovered.</b> Meaning, their protective shade departed from above them, for the pleasantness of HaShem had been upon them from the day they received the Torah, and their shelter was removed." "<b>For Aaron had let [them] loose.</b> Perhaps it intends to say that through Aaron's accounting [of the matter] the entire liability of the sin remained upon them, for Aaron did nothing of any substance regarding the calf — and everything was done by means of those who gave gold: they gave the gold and performed sorcery upon it to bring forth the calf that emerged, and Aaron did nothing but take the gold and cast it into the fire, and even that under duress, as he said: ״כִּי בְרָע הוּא״. From this point on, the entire sin is attributed to them and they have no covering of excuse, and this is what Scripture says — <b>for Aaron had let [them] loose</b> — meaning, through his argument." "<b>For derision among their enemies.</b> Every small measure, from the perspective of the abomination, is called a שֶׁמֶץ (trace). And our Sages of blessed memory said (Sanhedrin 102a): there is no trouble that befalls Israel that does not contain an element of this sin. Further, it intends that the nations who rise against us constantly have a retort in their mouths: ״You have already made yourselves abominable and been cast off.״"], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:36:09.198162866Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}